  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-2).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=3.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{\u2032}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-2) \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-2)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(3) & =2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3-2)+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{3-2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(3) & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{\u2032}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
